    "summary": "Scaffold a minimal Vite + vanilla-JS app showing \"BP03 contract\" and start the Vite dev server on port 8000.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "title": "Initialize Vite project with vanilla-JS template",
        "detail": "Run `npm create vite@latest . -- --template vanilla` to scaffold the project in the workspace root, then `npm install` to install dependencies."
      },
      {
        "title": "Set up the page content",
        "detail": "Edit index.html to include the heading \"BP03 contract\" and wire up main.js for a minimal app entry point."
      },
      {
        "title": "Start Vite dev server on port 8000",
        "detail": "Run `npx vite --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000` so the live preview is served from the Vite dev server (not static files)."
      }
    ],
    "revision": 1,
    "context": "## Findings\n- The workspace is completely empty \u2014 no existing files or config.\n\n## Approach\n- Use Vite's official `vanilla` template for the smallest possible setup (no frameworks, just plain JS + HTML).\n- The heading \"BP03 contract\" will go directly in `index.html` so it's visible immediately.\n- Starting Vite on port 8000 ensures the user preview is the live dev server with HMR support.\n\n## Trade-offs\n- Using `npm create vite` in-place (`.`) keeps everything flat in the workspace root, which is simplest for this minimal case."
  },
